    meta: 'Tannaitic *baraita* formula (“It was taught…”) preserved in uncensored medieval Talmudic codices (Codex Hebraicus 95, Munich, AD 1342; Florence II.1.8–9, AD 1177; Karlsruhe Reuchlin 2)',
    noteMd: 'The ***baraita*** (early rabbinic teaching from the Tannaitic period, c. AD 10–220) in **b. Sanhedrin 43a**, edited centuries later in Sasanian Babylonia, presents **Yeshu** from a rabbinic standpoint as working wonders (characterized as “sorcery and enticing Israel”), gathering named disciples, and being **hanged on the Eve of Passover** (`בערב הפסח`; cf. John 19:14). The verb `תלאוהו` (“they hanged him”) is widely, though not universally, interpreted as reflecting crucifixion or post-execution suspension (cf. Deuteronomy 21:22–23; Luke 23:39; Acts 5:30; Galatians 3:13). Princeton Judaic scholar **Peter Schäfer** (*Jesus in the Talmud*, 2007, ch. 6) reads the passage as a satirical counter-narrative responding to Christian passion preaching rather than as an independent first-century record.',
    quote: 'On the eve of the Passover Yeshu was hanged. For forty days before the execution took place, a herald went forth and cried, “He is going forth to be stoned because he has practiced sorcery and enticed Israel to apostasy…”',
