    meta: 'Thallus (1st- or 2nd-c. AD Greek chronicler), paraphrased by Sextus Julius Africanus (c. AD 160–240), *Chronographiae* Fragment 93, preserved by the Byzantine chronicler George Syncellus (*Ecloga Chronographica*, c. AD 800)',
    noteMd: 'In a fragment of his *Chronographiae* (c. **AD 221**) preserved by George Syncellus, the Christian chronographer **Julius Africanus** reports that **Thallus**, in the third book of his *Histories*, called the darkness at Jesus’s crucifixion (**Mark 15:33**; **Luke 23:44–45**) a **solar eclipse**. Africanus objects that a solar eclipse cannot occur at the **full moon** of **Passover** (*14 Nisan*), and he adds a parallel citation from the second-century chronicler **Phlegon of Tralles** (*Olympiads* Book 13, Olympiad 202.4 = AD 32/33). Because Thallus’s work is lost and a proposed mid-first-century date (c. AD 52) rests on a contested emendation in **Josephus** (*Ant.* 18.167), historians divide: ancient historian **Paul L. Maier** (1991) and New Testament scholar **Robert E. Van Voorst** (*Jesus Outside the New Testament*, 2000) view Thallus as an early non-Christian witness to the passion-darkness tradition, whereas mythicist historian **Richard Carrier** (*JGRChJ* 8, 2011–2012) argues that Thallus may have recorded an ordinary eclipse, dated by Olympiad, that was first connected to the Gospel passion by Africanus.',
    quote: 'On the whole world there pressed a most fearful darkness; and the rocks were rent by an earthquake, and many places in Judea and other districts were thrown down. This darkness Thallus, in the third book of his History, calls, as appears to me without reason, an eclipse of the sun. For the Hebrews celebrate the passover on the 14th day according to the moon, and the passion of our Saviour falls on the day before the passover; but an eclipse of the sun takes place only when the moon comes under the sun.',
